db/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308782 1111 #uuid "42cba7d3-fb52-5e3f-83ba-8aaf5a88dbb1" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308783 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308784 13194139534766 true] [17592186308783 1089 "q-120725" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308783 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534766 true] [17592186308783 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/d8bbcd878edaef5d3f5b92d106a071444f20c72f3d419689d15644a4842a2d45.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308783 1113 #uuid "fffb59d3-cca6-5381-9439-7548b7d65a74" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308783 1145 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{3},-3).$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308785 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308786 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308787 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308788 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308789 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1041 "selection" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308785 13194139534766 true] [17592186308784 1110 #uuid "5ef17ec4-4afd-5919-90a6-4f63cd444f4b" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308785 1034 "(3\\sqrt{3},-3)" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308785 1111 #uuid "77edfa21-9e9f-5abb-9b50-8ca611839d7c" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308786 1034 "(3\\sqrt{3},-3\\sqrt{3})" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308786 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308786 1111 #uuid "41fd0d7f-89bc-5171-846b-81958af77d2a" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308787 1034 "(-3\\sqrt{3},3\\sqrt{3})" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308787 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308787 1111 #uuid "3b4012e0-c379-5ac2-91b4-f8ca77534215" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308788 1034 "(-3,3)" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308788 1111 #uuid "2e3a311e-a831-5d22-8734-c423e776099f" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308789 1034 "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308789 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308789 1111 #uuid "568d2bb1-4f45-517d-ab45-68ee966db7c4" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308790 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308791 13194139534766 true] [17592186308790 1089 "q-17161" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308790 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534766 true] [17592186308790 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/11a75207b0adee9fad857aa93f15772e730716b7cc9d5fae7ba39fc5f55418f0.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(5,-5\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139534766 true] [17592186308790 1113 #uuid "88a106f3-b03f-57fc-a519-6c763e438e5c" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308790 1145 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-5\\sqrt{3}}{5}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308792 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308793 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308794 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308795 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308796 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1041 "selection" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308795 13194139534766 true] [17592186308791 1110 #uuid "e0b41517-9d29-5c76-bdc3-9114bb649974" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308792 1034 "\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308792 1111 #uuid "895369e6-c16e-5d29-969e-446a6c49885d" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308793 1034 "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308793 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308793 1111 #uuid "bc830c5b-fe18-5dbf-ac4e-f7b8c5a9315d" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308794 1034 "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308794 1111 #uuid "020f1180-8560-5ae8-8ac6-1f89bc6fc69b" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308795 1034 "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308795 1111 #uuid "5158bbeb-ba70-50b3-8da6-8b415b43f5eb" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308796 1034 "\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308796 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308796 1111 #uuid "c11d7e5c-bc8f-5de0-a2f6-d8cc1cc06db0" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308797 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308798 13194139534766 true] [17592186308797 1089 "q-274767" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308797 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534766 true] [17592186308797 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/1fe85402830f5976b8de6613464c358a47c185869d44a495f1efa9caed73e04c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534766 true] [17592186308797 1113 #uuid "3e5a84bb-82a4-53ae-95b9-861c6b48cf19" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308797 1145 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-5\\sqrt{3}}{-5}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308798 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308799 13194139534766 true] [17592186308798 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534766 true] [17592186308798 1041 "field-1" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308798 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308799 13194139534766 true] [17592186308798 1110 #uuid "4bbda075-92a3-5513-a664-87f32678d35f" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308799 1034 "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308799 1111 #uuid "60949875-ad4b-5d7e-ab1f-1f75869d5a6b" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308800 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308801 13194139534766 true] [17592186308800 1089 "q-321384" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308800 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534766 true] [17592186308800 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/afa5a80d73d9d8511cc35804277ad9dc3f1b62b2e1dbe3911d6ea9d665c3baa8.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(5,3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$ \n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ {{field-1}} \n$).$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308800 1113 #uuid "e6c959a6-46d2-5e2c-9bea-847c805ea0c6" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308800 1145 "The angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, the polar equation is  \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,).\n$$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308802 13194139534766 true] [17592186308801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308803 13194139534766 true] [17592186308801 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534766 true] [17592186308801 1041 "field-1" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308801 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308802 13194139534766 true] [17592186308801 1110 #uuid "b65a82ee-d706-5de7-96a5-d41f5b94957d" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308802 1034 "\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308802 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308802 1111 #uuid "7edd8c0e-8e59-571a-a4e3-20a186958b60" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308803 1034 "\\frac{5}{3}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308803 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308803 1111 #uuid "ac3e5c0e-011f-5839-b56d-99e4aa68acd6" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308804 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308805 13194139534766 true] [17592186308804 1089 "q-321898" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308804 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534766 true] [17592186308804 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/b07f18c13e3bca94d2b55d61b4a63b2a294d0e47ed8a2853f5e10c5140047b27.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-3\\sqrt{3},-3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534766 true] [17592186308804 1113 #uuid "c1a116a1-5be2-57cd-ab23-bacb48cabc85" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308804 1145 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308805 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308806 13194139534766 true] [17592186308805 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534766 true] [17592186308805 1041 "field-1" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308805 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308806 13194139534766 true] [17592186308805 1110 #uuid "db1ca8dc-ee50-5109-95c9-e3c56aa97366" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308806 1034 "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308806 1111 #uuid "4f845f32-0b5d-5d33-85cf-fe109b255eaa" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308807 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308808 13194139534766 true] [17592186308807 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308810 13194139534766 true] [17592186308807 1089 "q-321972" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308807 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534766 true] [17592186308807 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/6aeb571c312e4dcc0f9ffc4fe2ad9b1a1db5cf02a2f757b66191e31337a8c498.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6\\sqrt{2}$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308807 1113 #uuid "bcaa98ac-5620-5ad5-b3d4-f9e5ed073000" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308807 1145 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6,6).$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308808 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308809 13194139534766 true] [17592186308808 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534766 true] [17592186308808 1041 "field-1" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308808 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308809 13194139534766 true] [17592186308808 1110 #uuid "60832ce1-9940-558e-bb8a-ac1fbd44495f" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308809 1034 "-6" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308809 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308809 1111 #uuid "23437580-af33-5532-8b28-12b8403481dd" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308811 13194139534766 true] [17592186308810 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534766 true] [17592186308810 1041 "field-2" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308810 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308811 13194139534766 true] [17592186308810 1110 #uuid "590fa078-5e18-5f15-917a-7849ff5b53bf" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308811 1034 "6" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534766 true] [17592186308811 1111 #uuid "8244dc1f-5f7f-53f3-82e9-825735b1aac3" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308812 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308813 13194139534766 true] [17592186308812 1089 "q-40913" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308812 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534766 true] [17592186308812 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/19f94b8985a4b8da7899f984e0e1c3f88668aa59531658cd0118edb35aab1107.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139534766 true] [17592186308812 1113 #uuid "b2490317-067b-513c-9277-23bae0b21e71" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308812 1145 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{-6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139534766 true] [17592186308813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308814 13194139534766 true] [